Bubble Sort is a simple sorting algorithm that works by repeatedly swapping adjacent elements if they are in the wrong order. The algorithm gets its name from the way smaller elements "bubble" to the top of the array. Here is an implementation of Bubble Sort in Scala:
```
def bubbleSort(arr: Array[Int]): Array[Int] = {
 for (i <- 0 until arr.length - 1) {
 for (j <- 0 until arr.length - i - 1) {
 if (arr(j) > arr(j + 1)) {
 val temp = arr(j)
 arr(j) = arr(j + 1)
 arr(j + 1) = temp
 }
 }
 }
 arr
}
```
The `bubbleSort` function takes an array of integers `arr` as input and returns the sorted array. 
The first loop iterates over the array `arr` from the first element to the second-to-last element. This is because the last element will already be in its correct position after the first pass through the array. 
The second loop iterates over the array from the first element to the second-to-last element minus the number of iterations in the outer loop. This is because the largest element will "bubble up" to the end of the array after each pass through the inner loop, so we don't need to compare it again.
Inside the inner loop, we compare each adjacent pair of elements. If they are in the wrong order, we swap them using a temporary variable `temp`.
After both loops complete, the array is sorted and we return it.
As for potential modifications to improve efficiency or handle larger arrays, one approach would be to add a flag variable to check if any swaps were made during a pass through the array. If no swaps were made, we know the array is already sorted and we can exit the loop early. This can save time for arrays that are already partially sorted.
Another approach would be to use a different sorting algorithm altogether, such as Quick Sort or Merge Sort, which have better average-case time complexity than Bubble Sort. However, these algorithms are more complex to implement and may not be necessary for smaller arrays.
In terms of data structures, there are other sorting algorithms that utilize data structures such as heaps or trees to sort elements. These algorithms can be more efficient for larger arrays but may also be more complex to implement.

