In C++, you can differentiate between relative and absolute paths on a computer by understanding their differences in usage in a command line interface. 
An absolute path is a complete path that specifies the location of a file or directory from the root directory of the file system. It starts with a forward slash (/) on Unix-based systems or a drive letter (e.g., C:) on Windows-based systems. Absolute paths provide a complete reference to the location of a file or directory and are used when you need to specify the exact location of a file or directory.
A relative path, on the other hand, is a path that is relative to the current working directory of the user. It does not start with a forward slash or a drive letter. Instead, it specifies the location of a file or directory relative to the current working directory. Relative paths are used when you need to specify the location of a file or directory relative to the current working directory.
Here's a demonstration illustrating these paths using [Shell cmd] data format as an example in the C++ code:
```
